detail.abstract:
Introduction: Natural gas dehydration is essential for preventing hydrate formation, corrosion, and product-gas quality failures in gas-processing facilities. The Atuabo Gas Processing Plant (AGPP) in Ghana currently uses a monoethylene glycol (MEG)-based dehydration system, but the unit operates with a narrow compliance margin under variable inlet and regeneration conditions.&nbsp;
<div>
 Materials and methods: A steady-state Aspen HYSYS model of the glycol absorption-regeneration loop was developed using the Cubic-Plus-Association (CPA) equation of state. The model represented a free-water knockout separator, a 15-stage counter-current absorber, rich-glycol pressure letdown, a lean/rich glycol heat exchanger, a 10-stage regeneration column with a kettle reboiler, a recirculation pump, and a recycle block. MEG, diethylene glycol (DEG), and triethylene glycol (TEG) were compared using the same feed-gas composition, equipment geometry, and convergence criteria. Sensitivity analyses were performed for reboiler temperature, glycol circulation rate, and feed-gas inlet pressure.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Results: All three solvents met the 4 lb MMSCF-1 water-content specification at base-case conditions, but with different margins. DEG produced the lowest sales-gas water content of 1.056 lb MMSCF-1, the highest dehydration efficiency of 94.44%, and the lowest predicted sales-gas water dew point of 22.64 degrees C, with a reboiler duty of 3,750 kW. TEG achieved 1.891 lb MMSCF-1 at 5,167 kW, whereas MEG achieved 3.571 lb MMSCF-1 at 1,875 kW but became vulnerable below approximately 63 bar(g).&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Conclusions: The findings indicate that DEG offers the best balance between dehydration performance, regeneration-energy demand, and operational resilience for the AGPP under the evaluated operating basis. MEG remains energy-efficient but is constrained by thermal stability and compliance margin, whereas TEG is technically suitable but less favourable at the selected 200 degrees C regeneration condition.
</div>
